    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my security+ and im a bit stuck. if i am already hashing a password, why do i actually need to add a salt to it? wouldnt the hash alone be enough to keep it secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Adding a salt to a password before hashing it is crucial for security. While a hash alone can provide some level of security, it is vulnerable to rainbow table attacks, where attackers use precomputed tables of hash values to reverse encrypt a given hash to its original string without needing traditional brute-force attacks. Salts add an extra layer of complexity, making it much harder for attackers to generate a valid password-hash pair, as they would have to compute hash values for every possible password with every possible salt, significantly increasing the difficulty of successful attacks.",
