    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i get that rainbow tables are a problem, but can you explain how the salt actually breaks the precomputed table part? like, how does the presence of that extra random string stop someone who already has a list of hashes for common passwords like 'password123'?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely. The salt prevents the use of precomputed tables in a few ways:\n\n\n1. Unique Hashes: With salt, even if two users have the same password, they will have different hashes. This means the same password won't result in the same hash twice, making precomputed tables useless since they'd have to create a separate table for each possible salt.\n\n\n2. Large Number of Salt Possibilities: Salts can be long and random, which means that attackers would need to create vastly more precomputed tables to cover every possible salt combination. This becomes impractical for those tables, as computing and storing all possible salt-hash combinations is resource-intensive.\n\n\n3. Computational Feasibility: For empirical salt values, the attacker would need to calculate a hash for every possible salt for each password in their table, a process that would take a terrifying amount of time and computational power.\n\n\nAs a result, the presence of salt fundamentally disrupts any approach that relies on the use of precomputed tables, forcing attackers to either generate a new table for every potential salt which contradicts the purpose of needing such a table in the first place, or to carry out time-consuming brute-force attacks, both of which are considered largely impractical.",
